ws  if you look at the epidemic curve of  that data it really shows we've reached  our peak several weeks ago  and that correlates to our  rise in admissions to hospital which  peaked in the third week of january and  they've been coming down ever since so  yes they're plateauing  but they're plateauing at a very low  level  and hence i do think that the the peak  and risk and the peak inactivity of  omicron is behind us there remains a  risk there remains a risk of  transmission every day and hence the  need to continue to wear our mass at  present but we'll we'll review the  evidence of when we can further reduce  that public health measure of masking in  public spaces  and everyone should make a risk  assessment especially if you're  vulnerable to this virus if you're  elderly or immune suppressed  please continue to come forward and get  vaccinated as it protects against all  the severe outcomes but  i think the worst is behind us but there  is an ongoing risk i don't think uh and  and i've had conversations with those  experts from the science table will have  a significant rebound in activity or  impact on our health care system i do  think the worst is behind us  next question  your next question comes from charlie  pinkerton with queen's park briefing  please go ahead  good afternoon uh dr moore um  as part of the government's plan to end  its mandatory proof vaccination system  on  um march the 1st i'm of the  understanding that  the vaccine mandates and soft vaccine  mandates for  other professions  will continue  have you begun thinking about when these  should be ended  um and you know if so could you sort of  explain your where you currently stand  um on on these requirements  well thanks very much for that question  yes we're reviewing all directives and  letters of instruction that we put in  place  across multiple sectors in ontario  we do  think the the highest risk is behind us  that we're heading into a lower risk  environment  and that the need  