munization  for other venues so  i think it's timely to remove them for  businesses it there may be mandates uh  by organizations that are  not government  that would want to have a testing  strategy uh continue especially if they  have a high risk uh setting uh and i'll  just also be particular that the that  there was a mandate uh for vaccination  in long-term care facilities by the  minister  that is separate as it wasn't a  directive by  by the chief medical officer health  office  next question  your next question comes from holly  mackenzie suiter with the canadian press  please go ahead  hi dr moore i want to talk about the the  pace of the reopening plan the latest  version had  21 days between each step and you've  said repeatedly that's a good and  cautious way to monitor the impacts of  each step but now we're less than two  weeks away before all capacity limits  and the vaccine certificates end we're  not going to have that much time to  monitor the impacts of the changes today  so i could just explain what the rush is  with this especially looking at the the  wastewater data  wastewater data has come down  significantly so  we we are now if you recall we put  public health measures in on january 5th  we then opened schools on the 17th so  there was roughly you know 12 12 day  separation uh we then further opened the  economy again on the 31st um so another  two week separation we then made a  17-day separation so we're always  following the data and the science i'm  just so pleased  that our hospitalization rates are  coming down very rapidly they went up  rapidly and and now they're coming down  in a very quick descent and remembering  hospitalization is a later signal  so i do think the risk is less at a  population level i i do think we're the  worst is behind us uh and i think  following the data  any public health measure should be as  short as possible it limits people's  freedoms and rights and we have to be  responsive uh to to that and the  implications of public health measures  so we've always said that they should be  proportionate to the risk and balanced  in their response  and i think uh  shortening it by three to four days this  time from the 21st to the 17th was  reasonable uh and and because we're  having such a rapid descent and i do  anticipate that we won't have any  significant rebound in risk  to hospitalization that we can continue  at a two-week march  to march first  to open up more broadly  follow-up  yeah i'm just learning as well about um  you know last year we lifted  restrictions at around this time with a  plan to  not enforce them province wide again and  then in a couple months we had a hard  lockdown basically in april because of  the pressures on the health system  and i know you said you know we have  vaccines now things are different but  what's the plan in the event that there  is a new new variant that strains the  health system again like the unexpected  can happen as we know so  what is the the backup plan would  restrictions have been brought back or  are we looking regionally or kind of  what  um yeah can you just explain more the  thinking of kind of if things don't go  the way we want or these trends don't  continue  well i am optimistic um that we're on  the right path uh and want to thank all  ontarians for the sacrifices they've  made uh you're absolutely right we're in  a much better position now with 90 of  our 12 and up population having had at  least two doses 6.7 million ontarians  having had three doses and now having  wide availability of other therapeutics  including outpatient oral treatments  much better position now  i  absolutely don't want to have to go back  and  and impose any public health measures  you're basically asking are we ready if  there's a new variant we've got a very  robust surveillance system we can  expand our pcr testing capacity if  necessary  to try to deal with a new strain or  variant um we've got plenty of public  health experienc